<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove that for any sequence of binary alphabets <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{1},\mathcal{A}_{2},\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{1},\mathcal{A}_{2},\dots</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a cube-free word <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}c_{2}\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}c_{2}\dots</annotation></semantics></math> so that <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="c_{1}\in\mathcal{A}_{1},c_{2}\in\mathcal{A}_{2},\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}\in\mathcal{A}_{1},c_{2}\in\mathcal{A}_{2},\dots</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, for every <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, there are at least <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.35^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>1.35</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.35^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> cube-free words in <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{1}\times\mathcal{A}_{2}\times\dots\times\mathcal{A}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{1}\times\mathcal{A}_{2}\times\dots\times\mathcal{A}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. We also prove that if the list of alphabets is computable then one of these words is computable and its <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th letter can be computed in time polynomial in <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">square</em> (<span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">resp.</span> a <em id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cube</em>) is a word <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uu</annotation></semantics></math> (<span id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">resp.</span> <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uuu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uuu</annotation></semantics></math>) for some nonempty word <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. A <em id="S1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cube-free word</em> (resp. <em id="S1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">square-free word</em>) is a word that contains no cube (resp. square) as a factor.
Thue proved that there exists an infinite cube-free word over the binary alphabet and an infinite square-free word over the ternary alphabet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. These results are regarded as the first results in combinatorics on words, and many generalizations of these problems have been considered.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One such generalization is the notion of nonrepetitive coloring of graphs introduced by Alon et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. A graph coloring (of the edges or of the vertices) is said to be <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">nonrepetitive</em> if the sequence of colors along any path avoids squares (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> for a recent survey on this topic). This notion naturally led to the notion of nonrepetitive list-coloring, where instead of having one fixed set of colors every vertex has to choose a color from a list of colors specific to this vertex. The <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Thue-list number</em> of a graph is the smallest integer <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> such that if all the lists have size at least <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> then the graph can be nonrepetitively colored in such a way that each vertex receives a color from its list. In this article, we will consider a variant of the following challenging conjecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1</span></span><span id="Thmconjecture1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, …]</cite>)<span id="Thmconjecture1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Thue list number of the infinite path is <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a formulation of this conjecture in terms of combinatorics on words. By an <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">alphabet</em>, we mean any finite set. Given a sequence <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> of alphabets, we say that a word <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=w_{1}\ldots w_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=w_{1}\ldots w_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">respects</em> <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, if for all <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}\in\mathcal{A}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}\in\mathcal{A}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (the definition naturally extends to infinite words). Conjecture <a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="Conjecture 1 ([, …]). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can be rephrased as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 2</span></span><span id="Thmconjecture2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a sequence of alphabets <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A_{i}|\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A_{i}|\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an infinite square-free word that respects <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Without loss of generality, we can assume that all the <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are sets of integers, so we adopt this convention for the sake of notation in the rest of the article. It was first proven by Grytczuk, Przybyło, and Zhu that the conjecture is true if the condition <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{A}_{i}|\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{A}_{i}|\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> is replaced by <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{A}_{i}|\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{A}_{i}|\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.
The second author recently proved that if <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\bigcup_{i\geq 1}\mathcal{A}_{i}|=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\bigcup_{i\geq 1}\mathcal{A}_{i}|=4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{A}_{i}|\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{A}_{i}|\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> then there exists an infinite square-free word that respects <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. This result is far from proving the conjecture, but the author argues that if it were not for the computational limitations, the technique used in this could certainly prove the full conjecture. Indeed, this technique requires one to verify by a computer-assisted proof some growth property of a very large automaton associated with square-free words.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the current article, we consider a natural variant of the question. Since cubes are avoidable over the binary alphabet, is it possible to avoid cubes if all of the lists are of size <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>? We give a positive answer to this question in the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any sequence of binary alphabet <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{1},\mathcal{A}_{2},\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{1},\mathcal{A}_{2},\dots</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an infinite cube-free word in <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{1}\times\mathcal{A}_{2}\times\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{1}\times\mathcal{A}_{2}\times\dots</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, we do not prove this result directly, but we prove two different stronger results, each implying Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
These two proofs share a key idea: we can use an automaton to study the variant of the problem where we only forbid cubes of length at most <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, for some arbitrary choice of <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. Intuitively, short cubes are more difficult to avoid than long cubes, so by taking <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> large enough, we should obtain a good approximation of the problem. Adapting standard tools from combinatorics on words and automata theory, studying this approximation is quite simple to achieve.
The delicate part is then to prove that if <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is large enough this approximation is good enough to deduce something about the original problem where all cubes are forbidden.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The idea behind the first proof is to show that the growth rate of the language is not far from the growth rate of the regular approximation of the language which yields the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any sequence of binary alphabet <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{1},\mathcal{A}_{2},\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{1},\mathcal{A}_{2},\dots</annotation></semantics></math>, and for every <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist at least <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.35^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>1.35</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.35^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> cube-free words in <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{1}\times\mathcal{A}_{2}\times\dots\times\mathcal{A}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{1}\times\mathcal{A}_{2}\times\dots\times\mathcal{A}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The core idea allowing us to deduce bounds on the growth of a power-free language <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> from its approximation <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> was first used by Kolpakov to estimate the growth rate of power-free languages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Shur later improved upon this technique <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. This technique was later adapted by the second author to provide a partial answer to Conjecture <a href="#Thmconjecture1" title="Conjecture 1 ([, …]). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
The proof of this result applies the technique developed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> for squares, but provides a complete answer in the case of cubes.
The second proof of our main result does not imply anything about the number of solutions, but it contains algorithmic implications.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{1},\mathcal{A}_{2},\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{1},\mathcal{A}_{2},\dots</annotation></semantics></math> be a computable sequence of binary alphabet. Then there exists a computable infinite cube-free word <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=w_{1}w_{2}w_{3}\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=w_{1}w_{2}w_{3}\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{1}\times\mathcal{A}_{2}\times\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{1}\times\mathcal{A}_{2}\times\dots</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, there is an algorithm that given <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1}\ldots w_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}\ldots w_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> computes <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> in time polynomial in <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">This proof also relies on computing properties of the same automaton, but then we use a different argument for adding the large cubes back.
Intuitively, the idea is to introduce a weight function that measures how difficult it is to extend a word, and to prove that under some condition if the weight is not too large then at least one of the extensions also has a small weight (which we can then use inductively to construct a word). This idea was originally due to Miller <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, and was recently generalized in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. However, the weight functions in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> are much simpler and in our article the precise definition of the function depends on the automaton. Our result is in fact even stronger, as everything still holds if for all <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> an opponent is allowed to choose the alphabet <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> after we choose <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Note that Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> both independently imply Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, but none of them seem to imply the other. We discuss the possible connection between this result in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Potential connection between the two proofs ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The article is organized as follows. In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we introduce some common notations. In particular, we define the language <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> that approximate the language of cube-free words, and we give the statement of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> that is later crucial to our two proofs. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 A polynomial-time algorithm to construct cube-free words ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we prove Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Cube-free words grow exponentially ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we prove Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Finally, we provide in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Computer-assisted proof of Lemma ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> a short description of the computation used to verify Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Approximation by a regular language</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach is to bound the difference between the number of cube-free words and the number of words that avoids all cubes up to a given length <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.
We let <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of all cube-free words.
For a given integer <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of words that avoid cubes of period at most <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a sequence of alphabets <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we let <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}[(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}[(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}]</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}[(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}[(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}]</annotation></semantics></math>) be the elements of <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>) that respect <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In order to work with <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> (and <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}[(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}[(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}]</annotation></semantics></math>), we consider the (pseudo)-automaton that recognizes this language. The alphabet (and set of forbidden factors) is infinite, so this is not a regular language. But since the language <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> is stable up to a permutation of the alphabet (and crucially, the set of forbidden factors up to permutation of the alphabet is finite), we can consider a (pseudo) finite automaton up to permutation of the alphabet.
For this, one may construct the states as being all suffixes of length <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3p-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, we will instead adapt a state construction from Shur <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, which proves to be more efficient (both for the computer verifications and for the proofs), and was already used in similar settings where the alphabets are not fixed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">minimal cube</em> is a cube that does not contain another cube as a proper factor.
A word is <em id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normalized</em> if it is lexicographically<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          To define a lexicographical order on the words, remember that we already assumed without loss of generality that
<math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{i}\subset\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{i}\subset\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span> the smallest of all the words obtained by a permutation of the alphabet.
Informally, the states are given by the proper prefixes of all the minimal cubes with period at most <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely, let <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of normalized proper prefixes of minimal cubes of period at most <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, we abuse the notations and also let <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(w)</annotation></semantics></math> be the longest word from <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> that is a suffix of <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> up to a permutation of the alphabet.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">One easily verifies the following property of <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every word <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and every letter <math id="Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(ua)=\Gamma(\Gamma(u)a)\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(ua)=\Gamma(\Gamma(u)a)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">So we can think of <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> as the set of states of the automaton<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          If we really wanted to make this an automaton we should add that the initial state is given by <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, every state is an accepting state. In fact, from every state there is at most one transition with any given label, so it is almost a deterministic automaton except that some edges are missing.</span></span></span> recognizing <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> and for every state <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, the transition corresponding to the letter <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is simply given by <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(sa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(sa)</annotation></semantics></math> (up to a permutation of the alphabet there are finitely many possible values for <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>: it is either one of the letter already appearing in <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, or a new letter).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Using computer verifications that we detail in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Computer-assisted proof of Lemma ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we can obtain the following lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=12</annotation></semantics></math>. There exist nonnegative weights <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda_{s})_{s\in\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda_{s})_{s\in\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\varepsilon}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\varepsilon}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for every word <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> over the alphabet <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math>
and every <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}\in\binom{\mathbb{N}}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}\in\binom{\mathbb{N}}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\lambda_{\Gamma(w)}\leq\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}c\in\mathcal{A}\\
wc\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}\end{subarray}}\lambda_{\Gamma(wc)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\lambda_{\Gamma(w)}\leq\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}c\in\mathcal{A}\\
wc\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}\end{subarray}}\lambda_{\Gamma(wc)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1.457" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.457</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1.457</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">It should be noted that this lemma is verified for the exact value <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1.457" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.457</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1.457</annotation></semantics></math> which is a rational number and not with some floating point approximation of <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Any reference to <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in the remainder of the article refers to these particular <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=12</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1.457" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.457</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1.457</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We now prove a second property of <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> that we use in both proofs. First, we recall Fine and Wilf Theorem. An integer <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be a <em id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">period</em> of a word <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=u_{1}\ldots,u_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=u_{1}\ldots,u_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> if for all <math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in\{1,\ldots,k-p\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in\{1,\ldots,k-p\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{j}=u_{j+p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{j}=u_{j+p}</annotation></semantics></math> .</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Fine, Wilf)<span id="Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> are periods of a word <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|u|\geq i+j-\operatorname{gcd}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|u|\geq i+j-\operatorname{gcd}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{gcd}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{gcd}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is also a period of <math id="Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 7</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is a letter such that</span></p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="va\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">va\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="va=uyy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">va=uyy</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for some words </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|y|&gt;p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|y|&gt;p</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then <math id="Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(va)=\Gamma(yy)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(va)=\Gamma(yy)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the sake of contradiction, assume <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Gamma(va)|\geq|yy|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Gamma(va)|\geq|yy|</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition,
<math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(va)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(va)</annotation></semantics></math> is the prefix of a cube of period at most <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, so there exists some positive <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leq p</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is a period of <math id="S2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(va)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(va)</annotation></semantics></math>, hence a period of <math id="S2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="yy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">yy</annotation></semantics></math>.
Both <math id="S2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|y|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|y|</annotation></semantics></math> are periods of <math id="S2.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="yy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">yy</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|yy|=2|y|&gt;p+|y|\geq i+|y|&gt;i+|y|-\operatorname{gcd}(|y|,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0em">+</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|yy|=2|y|&gt;p+|y|\geq i+|y|&gt;i+|y|-\operatorname{gcd}(|y|,i)</annotation></semantics></math>. By Fine and Wilf’s theorem, <math id="S2.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="yy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">yy</annotation></semantics></math> also has period <math id="S2.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{gcd}(|y|,i)&lt;\frac{|y|}{2}=\frac{2|y|}{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{gcd}(|y|,i)&lt;\frac{|y|}{2}=\frac{2|y|}{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that <math id="S2.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="yy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">yy</annotation></semantics></math> (and <math id="S2.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="va" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">va</annotation></semantics></math>) contains a <math id="S2.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>-power, hence <math id="S2.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> contains a cube, which contradicts <math id="S2.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This result can be improved by using sharper versions of the inequalities at the cost of complicating the argument.
In particular, here we partly exploit the existence of a cube and only use the existence of a square, which is a weaker condition.
It is not clear how the resulting improvement could simplify the computation. Although it is not critical for our computation with <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=12</annotation></semantics></math>, improving such lemmas could be useful for other applications of the same techniques (e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, we will use the following consequence in the proofs of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 8</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is a letter such that</span></p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="va\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">va\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="va=uyyy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">va=uyyy</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for some non-empty words </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then <math id="Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(va)=\Gamma(yy)=\Gamma(uyy)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(va)=\Gamma(yy)=\Gamma(uyy)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Corollary 8. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and its proof can be seen as a particular case of a similar lemma used by Shur in the context of obtaining bounds for the growth of power-free languages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, Lemma 2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>A polynomial-time algorithm to construct cube-free words</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any word <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the weight <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(u)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> as follows,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\omega(u)=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}v\text{ such that: }\\
uv\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}\\
uv=xyyy\text{ with }|y|\geq 1,|v|&lt;|y|\end{subarray}}\lambda_{\Gamma(uv)}\beta^{-|v|}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>v</mi><mtext> such that: </mtext></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> with </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(u)=\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}v\text{ such that: }\\
uv\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}\\
uv=xyyy\text{ with }|y|\geq 1,|v|&lt;|y|\end{subarray}}\lambda_{\Gamma(uv)}\beta^{-|v|}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=12</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1.457" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.457</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1.457</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\Gamma(uv)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\Gamma(uv)}</annotation></semantics></math> are defined in Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. This quantity is parameterized by <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, and the particular choice of <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> will be fixed later.
Note that <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> can be <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, this weight attempts to evaluate how difficult it will be to avoid cubes when extending <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. For this, we consider cubes that are obtained by extending the word while remaining in <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> (in some sense, it measures how likely it is that a long cube appears if we randomly follow the automaton). We only consider cubes such that at least two periods of the cube are contained in the suffix of <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> (which will allow us to use Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Corollary 8. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). The more letters we have to add to create a specific cube, the less weight we want to give to this cube since we will have more opportunities to avoid it, which motivates the <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta^{-|v|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta^{-|v|}</annotation></semantics></math> term. On the other hand, a word <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> with a larger coefficient <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\Gamma(u)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\Gamma(u)}</annotation></semantics></math> has more extensions in <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence probably more extensions in <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> as well.
Multiplying by this coefficient <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\Gamma(u)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\Gamma(u)}</annotation></semantics></math> allows taking into account the fact that some words should be more costly to forbid than others because they have more extensions. Although we try to justify it by some intuitions, this definition is not simply a well-chosen heuristic, but a precise definition where every detail is crucial to the proof and can only be justified by the proof itself.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let us state the first simple property of this weight function.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 9</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> be a letter such that <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ua\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ua\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(ua)&lt;\lambda_{\Gamma(ua)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(ua)&lt;\lambda_{\Gamma(ua)}</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ua\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ua\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ua\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}\setminus\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ua\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}\setminus\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ua" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ua</annotation></semantics></math> admits a cube as a suffix. Hence, considering the first term of the sum in the definition of <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(ua)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(ua)</annotation></semantics></math> (for <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>) yields</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(ua)\geq\lambda_{\Gamma(ua\varepsilon)}\beta^{-|\varepsilon|}=\lambda_{\Gamma(ua)}\," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(ua)\geq\lambda_{\Gamma(ua\varepsilon)}\beta^{-|\varepsilon|}=\lambda_{\Gamma(ua)}\,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(\varepsilon)=0&lt;\lambda_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(\varepsilon)=0&lt;\lambda_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> (due to Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). The following lemma will be used to construct inductively a sequence <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> where all prefix <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is such that <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(u)&lt;\lambda_{\Gamma(u)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(u)&lt;\lambda_{\Gamma(u)}</annotation></semantics></math> so the whole sequence <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> by the previous lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 10</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> is such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta+\frac{\beta^{1-p}}{\beta-1}\leq\alpha." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta+\frac{\beta^{1-p}}{\beta-1}\leq\alpha.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be such that <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(u)&lt;\lambda_{\Gamma(u)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(u)&lt;\lambda_{\Gamma(u)}</annotation></semantics></math>, then for any alphabet <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> of size at least <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> there exists <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ua\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ua\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(ua)&lt;\lambda_{\Gamma(ua)}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(ua)&lt;\lambda_{\Gamma(ua)}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> be as in the theorem statement. By the definition of <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 4. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}c\in\mathcal{A}\\
uc\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}\end{subarray}}\lambda_{\Gamma(uc)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}c\in\mathcal{A}\\
