<div id="S3.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">In particular, with <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=12</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1.457" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.457</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1.457</annotation></semantics></math> we can set <math id="S3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1.35" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.35</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1.35</annotation></semantics></math> to satisfy the condition of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem10" title="Lemma 10. ‣ 3 A polynomial-time algorithm to construct cube-free words ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>.
So we inductively define <math id="S3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{0}=\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{0}=\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, and for all <math id="S3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{n+1}=u_{n}a_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{n+1}=u_{n}a_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n+1}\in\mathcal{A}_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n+1}\in\mathcal{A}_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen such that <math id="S3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(u_{n+1})&lt;\lambda_{\Gamma(u_{n+1})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(u_{n+1})&lt;\lambda_{\Gamma(u_{n+1})}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 9. ‣ 3 A polynomial-time algorithm to construct cube-free words ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, each of the <math id="S3.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is cube-free, and so is the infinite word <math id="S3.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1}a_{2}a_{3}\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1}a_{2}a_{3}\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> and by construction it belongs to <math id="S3.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{1}\times\mathcal{A}_{2}\times\mathcal{A}_{3}\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{1}\times\mathcal{A}_{2}\times\mathcal{A}_{3}\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> as desired.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Given <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, one only needs to compute the weight of <math id="S3.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{n}a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{n}a</annotation></semantics></math> for a given <math id="S3.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathcal{A}_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathcal{A}_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> to choose <math id="S3.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. For this, it is enough to be able to compute the set</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{v|u_{n}v\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)},u_{n}v=xyyy\text{ with }|y|&gt;p,|v|&lt;|y|\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mtext> with </mtext><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">&gt;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>v</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">&lt;</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v|u_{n}v\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)},u_{n}v=xyyy\text{ with }|y|&gt;p,|v|&lt;|y|\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">For any fixed choice of <math id="S3.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|v|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|v|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|y|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|y|</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is uniquely determined by <math id="S3.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then so is <math id="S3.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and one can verify in time <math id="S3.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(|y|)&lt;O(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(|y|)&lt;O(n)</annotation></semantics></math> if this choice of <math id="S3.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|v|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|v|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|y|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|y|</annotation></semantics></math> gives an element of this set (verifying if <math id="S3.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{n}v\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{n}v\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, if <math id="S3.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="yyy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">yyy</annotation></semantics></math> avoids cubes of period at most <math id="S3.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> can be done in time linear in <math id="S3.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|y|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|y|</annotation></semantics></math>). For the same reason, this set has size at most <math id="S3.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. We deduce that this set, and the weight <math id="S3.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(u_{n}a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(u_{n}a)</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed in time <math id="S3.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>. So in particular <math id="S3.p11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed in time <math id="S3.p11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> as desired.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the <math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> bound is not optimal. In fact, given <math id="S3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|y|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|y|</annotation></semantics></math> it takes <math id="S3.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(|y|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(|y|)</annotation></semantics></math> to find the smallest corresponding <math id="S3.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|v|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|v|</annotation></semantics></math> as it is equivalent to finding the largest suffix of <math id="S3.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of period <math id="S3.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|y|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|y|</annotation></semantics></math>. It is certainly possible to improve this complexity, in particular, if we allow ourselves to keep useful information between every addition of a letter (we are more interested in generating a word of size <math id="S3.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> than in being able to add one letter to a word of size <math id="S3.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Our argument implies something slightly stronger than Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Suppose that for every <math id="S3.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, you can learn <math id="S3.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> only after having chosen <math id="S3.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},\dots,a_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},\dots,a_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then we can still apply the exact same technique. Indeed, in our proof when we choose the letter <math id="S3.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we never make any assumption on the following alphabets, which is consistent with the aforementioned setting (in particular, note that the weight <math id="S3.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> does not depend on any of the alphabets, but only on the current word). Note that our second proof can also be adapted to say something about this setting, but it is not worth the extra technicalities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, the conclusion of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem10" title="Lemma 10. ‣ 3 A polynomial-time algorithm to construct cube-free words ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> can be reformulated as saying that there is at most one letter <math id="S3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega(ua)\geq\lambda_{\Gamma(ua)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega(ua)\geq\lambda_{\Gamma(ua)}</annotation></semantics></math>. This leads to an even stronger variant of the problem: instead of choosing the next letter an adversary provides an alphabet <math id="S3.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, we forbid one letter <math id="S3.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> allowing the adversary to append any chosen letter from <math id="S3.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\setminus a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\setminus a</annotation></semantics></math> to the current word. Our argument directly implies that it is possible for us to ensure that the resulting word avoid cubes for any strategy of the adversary. Furthermore, in this new setting, if <math id="S3.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is finite, the next move can always be computed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Cube-free words grow exponentially</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section is devoted to proving Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> on the exponential growth of the number of cube-free words of length <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> that respect given alphabets <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}_{1},\mathcal{A}_{2},\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}_{1},\mathcal{A}_{2},\dots</annotation></semantics></math>. For all <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of words in <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}\left[(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}\left[(\mathcal{A}_{i})_{i\geq 1}\right]</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. We will show</p>
<table id="S4.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C_{n}|\geq 1.35^{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>1.35</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C_{n}|\geq 1.35^{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For all <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of cube-free words from <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> that are in state <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, that is,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}^{t}=\{w\in C_{n}:\Gamma(w)=t\}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}^{t}=\{w\in C_{n}:\Gamma(w)=t\}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We first prove an inequality about the size of these sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 11</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the following inequality</span></p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C_{n+1}^{t}|\geq\sum_{v\in C_{n}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}c\in\mathcal{A}_{n+1}\\
vc\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}\\
\Gamma(vc)=t\end{subarray}}1-\sum_{i=p+1}^{n+1}|C_{n+1-i}^{t}|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C_{n+1}^{t}|\geq\sum_{v\in C_{n}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}c\in\mathcal{A}_{n+1}\\
vc\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}\\
\Gamma(vc)=t\end{subarray}}1-\sum_{i=p+1}^{n+1}|C_{n+1-i}^{t}|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We let <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of words of length <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1}\times\ldots\times A_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1}\times\ldots\times A_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> so that</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the prefix of size <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is cube-free,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the word does not contain any cubes of period at most <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the word ends in state <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">In other words,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="G^{t}=\left\{va\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}:v\in C_{n},a\in A_{n+1},\Gamma(va)=t\right\}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{t}=\left\{va\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}:v\in C_{n},a\in A_{n+1},\Gamma(va)=t\right\}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">Hence,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|G^{t}|=\sum_{v\in C_{n}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}c\in\mathcal{A}_{n+1}\\
vc\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}\\
\Gamma(vc)=t\end{subarray}}1\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|G^{t}|=\sum_{v\in C_{n}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}c\in\mathcal{A}_{n+1}\\
vc\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}\\
\Gamma(vc)=t\end{subarray}}1\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of words from <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> that contain a cube of period <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> as a suffix. By definition, a word from <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> can only contain a cube as a suffix and this cube must be of period more than <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> (and at most <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>).<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
              We could easily replace this <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="footnote3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor(n+1)/3\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor(n+1)/3\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>, but it does not make any difference in the latter part of the proof.</span></span></span> We get</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n+1}^{t}=G^{t}\setminus\bigcup_{i=p+1}^{n+1}F_{i}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">∖</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n+1}^{t}=G^{t}\setminus\bigcup_{i=p+1}^{n+1}F_{i}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.5.1" class="ltx_text">which implies</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|C_{n+1}^{t}|\geq|G^{t}|-\sum_{i=p+1}^{n+1}|F_{i}|\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|C_{n+1}^{t}|\geq|G^{t}|-\sum_{i=p+1}^{n+1}|F_{i}|\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.6.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to prove that <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|F_{i}|\leq|C_{n+1-i}^{t}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|F_{i}|\leq|C_{n+1-i}^{t}|</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to conclude the proof.
Let <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="va\in F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">va\in F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, then by definition <math id="S4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="va=uyyy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">va=uyyy</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|y|=i&gt;p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|y|=i&gt;p</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="va\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">va\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can apply Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Corollary 8. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> to deduce that <math id="S4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(uyy)=\Gamma(vc)=t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(uyy)=\Gamma(vc)=t</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, <math id="S4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uyy\in C_{n+1-i}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uyy\in C_{n+1-i}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given the word <math id="S4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uyy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uyy</annotation></semantics></math> and the period <math id="S4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=|y|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=|y|</annotation></semantics></math>, we can uniquely reconstruct <math id="S4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vc=uyyy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vc=uyyy</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies <math id="S4.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|F_{i}|\leq|C_{n+1-i}^{t}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|F_{i}|\leq|C_{n+1-i}^{t}|</annotation></semantics></math>, which concludes our proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that, by Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we have nonnegative weights <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> for the states <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> so that for every <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and every <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}\in\binom{\mathbb{N}}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}\in\binom{\mathbb{N}}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
