<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Computer-assisted proof of Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, the way we establish <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is inspired by the method of approximation of the spectral radius. We first explicitly compute the set of states <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. We initiate <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{s}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{s}=1</annotation></semantics></math> for every state <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. In each iteration, we follow (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Lemma 5. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) in Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> to reassign <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S5.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{s}=\min_{\mathcal{A}\in\binom{[2+\max s]}{2}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}c\in\mathcal{A}\\
sc\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}\end{subarray}}\lambda_{\Gamma(sc)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{s}=\min_{\mathcal{A}\in\binom{[2+\max s]}{2}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}c\in\mathcal{A}\\
sc\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}\end{subarray}}\lambda_{\Gamma(sc)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that the minimum is over <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{[2+\max s]}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{[2+\max s]}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, instead of <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{\mathbb{N}}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{\mathbb{N}}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Lemma 5. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), but they are equivalent. Indeed, whenever the letters of <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> do not appear in <math id="S5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, we can always assume by symmetry that they are <math id="S5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s|+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s|+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s|+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s|+2</annotation></semantics></math>.
We repeat iterations until we have (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Lemma 5. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) with</p>
<table id="S5.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\left\{\frac{1}{\lambda_{s}}\min_{\mathcal{A}\in\binom{[2+\max s]}{2}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}c\in\mathcal{A}\\
sc\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}\end{subarray}}\lambda_{\Gamma(sc)}:s\in\Gamma\right\}\geq 1.457\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></munder><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1.457</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\left\{\frac{1}{\lambda_{s}}\min_{\mathcal{A}\in\binom{[2+\max s]}{2}}\sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}c\in\mathcal{A}\\
sc\in\widetilde{\mathcal{C}}^{(p)}\end{subarray}}\lambda_{\Gamma(sc)}:s\in\Gamma\right\}\geq 1.457\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which implies that (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Lemma 5. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) holds with the current values of <math id="S5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda_{s})_{s\in\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda_{s})_{s\in\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>. It remains at this point to verify the condition <math id="S5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\varepsilon}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\varepsilon}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. All of the computation are done exactly using rational numbers which ensures that there is no problem of numerical approximations.
The program<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
            
            
            
          Note that this program requires 20,2GB of RAM and a few minutes to run with the required parameters.</span></span></span> is available at the link <a href="https://github.com/matthieu-rosenfeld/cube_free_over_list_of_size_2" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/matthieu-rosenfeld/cube_free_over_list_of_size_2</a></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned, earlier, updating <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> with the result of (<a href="#S5.E3" title="In 5 Computer-assisted proof of Lemma ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) is really similar to the way one can find the spectral radius of a matrix. We verified in practice that this process converges for this particular system (at least, it seems to converge up to a really good approximation), but there should be a more general theoretical reason for the convergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmquestion1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_question">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmquestion1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Question 1</span></span><span id="Thmquestion1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmquestion1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmquestion1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmquestion1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can we prove any guarantee on the convergence of this process?</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Potential connection between the two proofs</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As already mentioned in the introduction, Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> both independently imply Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, but none of them seem to imply the other one. There are obvious connections between these proofs exemplified by the fact that in both of them we use the same computer verification provided by Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 5. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, and we use Corollary <a href="#Thmtheorem8" title="Corollary 8. ‣ 2 Approximation by a regular language ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> to bound the number of bad extensions. But there seem to be even deeper connections between these two results as in particular, both proofs lead to two lemmas (respectively, Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem10" title="Lemma 10. ‣ 3 A polynomial-time algorithm to construct cube-free words ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#Thmtheorem13" title="Lemma 13. ‣ 4 Cube-free words grow exponentially ‣ There exist infinite cube-free words over any sequence of binary alphabets" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) that require exactly the same algebraic condition, the existence of a real <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S6.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta+\frac{\beta^{1-p}}{\beta-1}\leq\alpha." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>β</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta+\frac{\beta^{1-p}}{\beta-1}\leq\alpha.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We wonder whether it is possible to better understand the similarities between these two proofs, to maybe obtain a shorter proof that directly implies both results.</p>
</div>
</section>
